<h1>A short glossary of Gregorian chant</h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    All the key words met throughout the blog, gathered here and explained in one sentence.
    Keep it at hand as you read.
</p>

<h2>The styles of chant</h2>
<table class="grille">
    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>In plain words</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Syllabic</strong></td><td>One note per syllable. The text stays very clear. E.g.&nbsp;: the sequence <span class="mot-latin">Victimæ paschali</span>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Neumatic</strong></td><td>A few notes (2 to 4) per syllable. E.g.&nbsp;: the introit <span class="mot-latin">Puer natus est</span>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Melismatic</strong></td><td>Many notes on a single syllable. E.g.&nbsp;: the gradual <span class="mot-latin">Viderunt omnes</span>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Melisma</strong></td><td>The long garland of notes itself, sung on a vowel.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Jubilus</strong></td><td>The great melisma of joy, typically on the «&nbsp;a&nbsp;» of <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia</span>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

