<h2>The signs of nuance</h2>
<table class="grille">
    <thead><tr><th>Sign</th><th>What it asks for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Quilisma</strong></td><td>A light zigzag note, a springboard to the next note&nbsp;; the preceding note is slightly lengthened.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Episema</strong></td><td>A small stroke&nbsp;: the note is settled on, broadened.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Letter <span class="mot-latin">c</span></strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">celeriter</span> — quickly, without leaning.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Letter <span class="mot-latin">t</span></strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">tenete</span> — hold, lengthen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Letter <span class="mot-latin">a</span></strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">altius</span> — higher.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>A few additional definitions</h2>
<table class="grille">
    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>In plain words</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Neume</strong></td><td>A sign notating one or more sounds on the same syllable (from the Greek <span class="mot-latin">pneuma</span>, breath).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Staff / tetragram</strong></td><td>The four lines on which square notation is written.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Clef</strong></td><td>A marker at the start of the line (<em>C</em> or <em>F</em> clef) that locates the semitones.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Diastematic</strong></td><td>Notation that indicates exact pitches (square notation).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Adiastematic</strong></td><td>Notation «&nbsp;in the open field&nbsp;», showing the gesture without precise pitches (the old neumes).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong><span class="mot-latin">Litteræ significativæ</span></strong></td><td>The small letters added to the neumes to specify rhythm or expression.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mode</strong></td><td>The «&nbsp;family of notes&nbsp;» and the colour of a melody (eight modes in chant).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Graduale Triplex</strong></td><td>Book from Solesmes (1979) superimposing three notations&nbsp;: Laon (top), square notation (centre), St. Gall (bottom).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Laon (ms 239)</strong></td><td>9th-c. manuscript&nbsp;; <em>Messine</em> script, famous for its rhythmic precision.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>St. Gall</strong></td><td>Swiss manuscripts (<span class="mot-latin">Cantatorium</span> 359, Einsiedeln 121)&nbsp;; a clear script for the melodic gesture.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>The genres of chant encountered</h2>
<table class="grille">
    <thead><tr><th>Genre</th><th>Role in the Mass</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Introit</strong></td><td>Entrance chant. E.g.&nbsp;: <span class="mot-latin">Puer natus est</span>, <span class="mot-latin">Lux fulgebit</span>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gradual</strong></td><td>Meditative chant after the 1st reading. E.g.&nbsp;: <span class="mot-latin">Viderunt omnes</span>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sequence</strong></td><td>Strophic chant before the Gospel, on great feasts. E.g.&nbsp;: <span class="mot-latin">Victimæ paschali laudes</span>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Alleluia</strong></td><td>Acclamation before the Gospel, prolonged by the <em>jubilus</em>. E.g.&nbsp;: <span class="mot-latin">Dies sanctificatus</span>, <span class="mot-latin">Pascha nostrum</span>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">Going further</span>
    <p>
        The best teacher remains the ear&nbsp;: listen to recordings of these six chants
        (many monasteries share them freely) while following the text, then while following
        the neumes. Little by little, the drawing and the sound will come together.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Where they fit in the Mass</h2>
<p>
    These chants aren't interchangeable&nbsp;: each has its precise place in the order of
    the Mass. Two categories are traditionally distinguished&nbsp;:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>the <strong>Proper</strong> (<span class="mot-latin">Proprium</span>)&nbsp;: the text
        changes according to the feast of the day — this is where our six chants belong&nbsp;;</li>
    <li>the <strong>Ordinary</strong> (<span class="mot-latin">Ordinarium</span>)&nbsp;: the
        text stays the same all year round (Kyrie, Gloria, Credo, Sanctus, Agnus Dei…).</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Here is the order in which these pieces are sung during Mass&nbsp;:
</p>
<table class="grille">
    <thead><tr><th>Chant</th><th>Proper or Ordinary&nbsp;?</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Introit</strong></td><td>Proper</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Kyrie</strong></td><td>Ordinary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gloria</strong></td><td>Ordinary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gradual</strong> — followed, on certain feasts, by an <strong>Alleluia</strong>, a <strong>Tract</strong>, or a <strong>sequence</strong></td><td>Proper</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Credo</strong></td><td>Ordinary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Offertory</strong></td><td>Proper</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sanctus</strong></td><td>Ordinary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Agnus Dei</strong></td><td>Ordinary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Communion</strong></td><td>Proper</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ite missa est</strong></td><td>fixed text</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>
    Where to find these texts&nbsp;: the <strong>Proper</strong> is in the <em>Graduale
    Romanum</em> or the <em>Graduale Triplex</em> (Solesmes ed.), the <strong>Ordinary</strong>
    in the <em>Kyriale Romanum</em> (Solesmes ed.).
</p>

<h2>Chronology up to the First Crusade</h2>
<p>
    The key dates leading from ancient Christianity to the “Gregorian” chant as fixed by
    the Carolingian era, up to the eve of the First Crusade — the full story is told in
    <a href="{{ path('paleo_en_histoire') }}">What do we mean by «&nbsp;Gregorian&nbsp;»&nbsp;?</a>
</p>
<table class="grille">
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>313</td><td>Edict of Milan&nbsp;: Christianity becomes a lawful religion in the Roman Empire.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4th&nbsp;c. (c.&nbsp;386)</td><td>Ambrose of Milan introduces antiphonal singing in Milan (“Ambrosian” chant).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>590 – 604</td><td>Pontificate of Gregory the Great&nbsp;; reorganisation of the Roman liturgy and its <span class="mot-latin">schola cantorum</span>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>711</td><td>Beginning of the Muslim presence on the Iberian Peninsula (<span class="mot-latin">al-Andalus</span>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>732</td><td>Charles Martel halts the Saracen advance at Poitiers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>768 – 814</td><td>Reign of Charlemagne&nbsp;: Roman chant is imposed across the empire&nbsp;; its encounter with Frankish practice gives birth to the “Gregorian” repertoire. The unification of Gregorian chant begins with <i>Chrodegang</i>, bishop of Metz, who founds a <i>Schola Cantorum</i>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>800</td><td>Charlemagne is crowned emperor in Rome by the Pope.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>9th&nbsp;c. (c.&nbsp;850)</td><td>The first <strong>neumes</strong> appear&nbsp;: simple memory aids for the vocal gesture, without precise pitches (the Laon manuscript, Messine neumes).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>9th&nbsp;c.</td><td><strong>Kassia of Constantinople</strong>, Byzantine hymnographer and composer&nbsp;: one of the few medieval women composers whose music has survived.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>c.&nbsp;873</td><td>A <em>Life</em> of Gregory tells, for the first time, the legend of the dove of the Holy Spirit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10th&nbsp;c.</td><td>The <strong>Einsiedeln</strong> manuscript (Stiftsbibliothek, codex 121)&nbsp;: one of the oldest neumed witnesses (St. Gall notation) of Gregorian chant, recording among others the introit <a href="{{ path('paleo_en_puer') }}"><span class="mot-latin">Puer natus est</span></a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2nd half of the 10th&nbsp;c.</td><td>The first staff lines appear, to make the pitch of notes more precise (in Italy and Aquitaine).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1054</td><td>Schism between the Eastern and Western Churches. The two liturgies (Roman and Byzantine) develop independently from each other from then on.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>11th&nbsp;c.</td><td><strong>Square notation</strong> on a staff appears&nbsp;: pitches finally become clearly readable.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1066</td><td>Norman Conquest of England by William the Conqueror.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1095</td><td>Council of Clermont&nbsp;: Pope Urban&nbsp;II preaches the First Crusade.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1099</td><td>Capture of Jerusalem&nbsp;: end of the First Crusade.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Bibliography &amp; resources</h2>
<p>
    A selection for going further, from the most accessible to the most specialised.
</p>

<h3>Introductory works</h3>
<ul class="biblio">
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Saulnier, Daniel.</span> <em>Le chant grégorien.</em> Solesmes.
        <span class="ref-note">A short, clear and accessible synthesis&nbsp;: the ideal
        sequel to this blog.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Hiley, David.</span> <em>Gregorian Chant.</em> Cambridge
        University Press, 2009. <span class="ref-note">The standard introduction in English,
        highly pedagogical.</span>
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>Specialised works</h3>
<ul class="biblio">
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Cardine, Eugène.</span> <em>Sémiologie grégorienne.</em>
        Solesmes, 1970. <span class="ref-note">The founding book on reading the old neumes
        (St. Gall, Laon)&nbsp;; technical.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Apel, Willi.</span> <em>Gregorian Chant.</em> Indiana
        University Press, 1958. <span class="ref-note">A classic survey of history, genres
        and forms.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Solesmes Abbey.</span> <em>Paléographie Musicale</em> (series,
        from 1889). <span class="ref-note">Annotated manuscript facsimiles&nbsp;; the origin
        of the modern restoration.</span>
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>Chant editions</h3>
<ul class="biblio">
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Solesmes Abbey.</span> <em>Graduale Triplex.</em> Solesmes,
        1979 — and the <em>Offertoriale Triplex</em> (Solesmes, 1985) for the offertories
        and their verses. <span class="ref-note">The edition at the heart of this
        blog&nbsp;: square notation + neumes of Laon and St. Gall.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Solesmes Abbey.</span> <em>Graduale Romanum.</em> (Vatican
        edition). <span class="ref-note">The official gradual in square notation
        only.</span>
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>Digitised manuscripts &amp; online resources (freely accessible)</h3>
<ul class="biblio">
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">e-codices.</span> Virtual manuscript library of Switzerland.
        <span class="ref-note">Major witnesses from St. Gall&nbsp;: <em>Cantatorium</em> (ms
        359), Hartker antiphonary (ms 390/391), and Einsiedeln 121.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">Gallica.</span> Digital library of the BnF. <span
        class="ref-note">Many liturgical manuscripts, including the Laon tradition.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="auteur">GregoBase&nbsp;; Cantus Database.</span> Databases of scores
        and Gregorian chants. <span class="ref-note">To find, catalogue and transcribe a
        particular piece.</span>
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="encadre">
    <span class="etiquette">Listening</span>
